WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.153
Welcome to Module 7 of
the Data Platform and SQL

2
00:00:03.153 --> 00:00:06.425
Server Identity learning
path: the application chain.

3
00:00:06.425 --> 00:00:09.328
It goes with Runbook R oh
eight and Chapter 07 of

4
00:00:09.328 --> 00:00:12.963
the developer workbook,
Building SQL Server Identity.

5
00:00:12.963 --> 00:00:16.580
You'll find every connection
string, mask every stored password,

6
00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:20.401
and place each string on the database
axis before anything changes.

7
00:00:20.401 --> 00:00:23.630
Take this module after Modules 6 and 4.

8
00:00:27.430 --> 00:00:28.767
Why does this matter?

9
00:00:28.767 --> 00:00:30.755
The engine is only half the chain.

10
00:00:30.755 --> 00:00:32.919
The other half is a connection string,

11
00:00:32.919 --> 00:00:36.130
and most of them hold a password
or a Windows credential.

12
00:00:36.130 --> 00:00:38.298
Until each string moves to an Entra mode,

13
00:00:38.298 --> 00:00:42.042
the application keeps the instance
tied to Windows Authentication.

14
00:00:42.042 --> 00:00:46.826
In this module you list every sequel server
connection string an application holds,

15
00:00:46.826 --> 00:00:49.218
and say which ones
carry a stored password.

16
00:00:49.218 --> 00:00:52.821
Then you say whether each move
is a configuration change,

17
00:00:52.821 --> 00:00:56.655
a driver or code change, the
vendor's decision, or already done.

18
00:00:56.655 --> 00:00:59.620
No string changes until
the three gates hold.

19
00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:06.136
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

20
00:01:06.136 --> 00:01:08.420
which takes about fifty
minutes with the lab.

21
00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:11.625
You'll place a connection
string in column A, B,

22
00:01:11.625 --> 00:01:15.495
C or D of the database axis,
and say who decides column D.

23
00:01:15.495 --> 00:01:20.324
You'll name the managed identity and
service-principal modes in each driver family,

24
00:01:20.324 --> 00:01:23.333
and say why the Entra password
mode is never a target.

25
00:01:23.333 --> 00:01:26.205
You'll run the kit's
connection scan over fixtures

26
00:01:26.205 --> 00:01:29.007
and read its masked strings,
columns and targets.

27
00:01:29.007 --> 00:01:32.708
And you'll list the three gates that
must hold before a string changes.

28
00:01:32.708 --> 00:01:35.824
Before you start, finish
Module 6, and Module 4,

29
00:01:35.824 --> 00:01:38.857
for the Entra login that
gate one checks for.

30
00:01:38.857 --> 00:01:44.610
The lab runs in PowerShell 7, in the kit
you unzipped into the F M W Labs folder.

31
00:01:48.410 --> 00:01:50.798
Unit 2 is the database axis.

32
00:01:50.798 --> 00:01:55.038
The data platform book's chapter on
the four-class pattern for applications

33
00:01:55.038 --> 00:01:58.919
describes two independent axes: how
users sign in to the application,

34
00:01:58.919 --> 00:02:01.711
and how the application
connects to the database.

35
00:02:01.711 --> 00:02:06.410
On the database axis, evidence places
each application in one of four columns.

36
00:02:06.410 --> 00:02:08.335
A is configuration only.

37
00:02:08.335 --> 00:02:09.960
B is a code change.

38
00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:11.585
C is vendor-dependent.

39
00:02:11.585 --> 00:02:13.547
And D is no Entra path today.

40
00:02:13.547 --> 00:02:17.830
The kit's scan reads each
application's configuration files,

41
00:02:17.830 --> 00:02:22.369
masks every stored password, and sets
A, B, C or done, from the driver,

42
00:02:22.369 --> 00:02:26.823
its version, the host, and whether
a vendor owns the application.

43
00:02:26.823 --> 00:02:31.786
A string with no owner's file, or a driver
the table doesn't know, goes to review.

44
00:02:31.786 --> 00:02:33.735
The scan never sets column D.

45
00:02:33.735 --> 00:02:38.012
Only the owner can say a vendor has no
release coming or an application is

46
00:02:38.012 --> 00:02:42.660
to be retired, and that answer goes into
the exception registry, with a date.

47
00:02:46.460 --> 00:02:49.123
Unit 3 starts with what
Microsoft gives you.

48
00:02:49.123 --> 00:02:52.809
Each driver family added Entra
modes in specific releases.

49
00:02:52.809 --> 00:02:57.232
Microsoft Data SQL Client added
service principal in two point oh,

50
00:02:57.232 --> 00:03:01.197
managed identity in two point one,
and Default in three point oh.

51
00:03:01.197 --> 00:03:05.358
The O D B C driver added managed
identity in seventeen point three

52
00:03:05.358 --> 00:03:09.673
point one point one and service
principal in seventeen point seven.

53
00:03:09.673 --> 00:03:15.590
The table gives the O L E D B driver eighteen
point three and eighteen point five,

54
00:03:15.590 --> 00:03:18.322
and the J D B C driver twelve point two.

55
00:03:18.322 --> 00:03:21.665
In every family the Entra
password mode is deprecated,

56
00:03:21.665 --> 00:03:23.597
so it's never a migration target.

57
00:03:23.597 --> 00:03:28.137
Microsoft also says a token doesn't
grant database permissions by itself,

58
00:03:28.137 --> 00:03:33.197
and to prefer a specific mode in production,
such as managed identity, over Default.

59
00:03:33.197 --> 00:03:35.317
And on an Arc-enabled Windows server,

60
00:03:35.317 --> 00:03:39.909
a process can get the machine's managed
identity token only if it runs as a local

61
00:03:39.909 --> 00:03:44.880
administrator, or as a member of the
Hybrid Agent Extension Applications group.

62
00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:49.880
Then the change.

63
00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:52.683
The data platform book's
chapter on connection string

64
00:03:52.683 --> 00:03:55.980
migration gives it for an
application on an Arc-enabled server.

65
00:03:55.980 --> 00:04:01.064
Integrated Security becomes Authentication
equals Active Directory Managed Identity,

66
00:04:01.064 --> 00:04:05.605
and the application's process account must
be allowed to request the host's token.

67
00:04:05.605 --> 00:04:07.728
Off Arc there's no managed identity,

68
00:04:07.728 --> 00:04:12.193
so the kit names the service-principal
mode, with a certificate preferred.

69
00:04:12.193 --> 00:04:13.893
It never suggests Default.

70
00:04:13.893 --> 00:04:16.142
The change is simple, but gated.

71
00:04:16.142 --> 00:04:18.692
Gate one: the Entra login exists.

72
00:04:18.692 --> 00:04:20.905
Gate two: the role is granted.

73
00:04:20.905 --> 00:04:24.980
Gate three: the change is tested in
a production-equivalent environment.

74
00:04:24.980 --> 00:04:28.255
A string changed before the
gates hold fails at sign-in.

75
00:04:28.255 --> 00:04:33.090
And the scan's target is advice, not
a change: the kit rewrites nothing.

76
00:04:36.890 --> 00:04:41.440
Chapter 07 of the workbook names the
dangers, and the guard against each.

77
00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:46.018
A password copied into a report turns
the scan's output into a secret store,

78
00:04:46.018 --> 00:04:51.177
so the kit masks every password, and a test
checks that no fixture password appears.

79
00:04:51.177 --> 00:04:54.713
The password mode counted as
done would leave a deprecated

80
00:04:54.713 --> 00:04:58.177
flow that can't do M F A in
production, so the finding names it.

81
00:04:58.177 --> 00:05:02.361
Active Directory Default in production
makes the first connection slow,

82
00:05:02.361 --> 00:05:05.927
and the identity can change with
host settings, so name the mode.

83
00:05:05.927 --> 00:05:08.818
Every application on a host
shares the Arc identity,

84
00:05:08.818 --> 00:05:12.515
so one login's grants cover everything
allowed to ask for the token.

85
00:05:12.515 --> 00:05:16.748
Keep the Hybrid Agent Extension Applications
group to the accounts that need it,

86
00:05:16.748 --> 00:05:20.665
and give an application its own service
principal where its grants differ.

87
00:05:20.665 --> 00:05:24.286
And a string changed before the
gates hold fails at sign-in,

88
00:05:24.286 --> 00:05:27.470
so Lab 2 checks gates one
to three, in that order.

89
00:05:31.270 --> 00:05:32.157
Try it.

90
00:05:32.157 --> 00:05:37.258
Lab 1 runs in PowerShell 7 on the kit's four
fictional applications, with no tenant.

91
00:05:37.258 --> 00:05:40.763
Go to the kit's folder and make
this run's results folder,

92
00:05:40.763 --> 00:05:42.944
named for the time you start, in U T C.

93
00:05:42.944 --> 00:05:45.358
Step one scans the four applications.

94
00:05:45.358 --> 00:05:49.358
Two strings hold a stored password,
and one is already on Entra.

95
00:05:49.358 --> 00:05:53.783
Payroll web's web dot config string
uses Windows integrated authentication.

96
00:05:53.783 --> 00:05:58.989
It runs on an Arc host with Microsoft
Data SQL Client five point two point two,

97
00:05:58.989 --> 00:06:04.195
at or above the managed identity release,
so it's column A: a configuration change.

98
00:06:04.195 --> 00:06:07.163
Claims batch and reports
O D B C use drivers

99
00:06:07.163 --> 00:06:09.707
with no Entra path, so they're column B.

100
00:06:09.707 --> 00:06:12.845
Vendor H R is column C
because a vendor owns it.

101
00:06:12.845 --> 00:06:17.670
The target text there is the kit's generic
advice; the vendor's release decides.

102
00:06:21.470 --> 00:06:23.645
Step two reads the masked strings.

103
00:06:23.645 --> 00:06:25.707
No password reaches the output.

104
00:06:25.707 --> 00:06:28.794
The scan replaces every
password value with stars,

105
00:06:28.794 --> 00:06:31.733
and never returns or writes
the password itself.

106
00:06:31.733 --> 00:06:33.697
That's the guard against a danger:

107
00:06:33.697 --> 00:06:38.133
a password copied into a report turns
the scan's output into a secret store.

108
00:06:38.133 --> 00:06:43.320
Look at the two stored passwords: claims
batch's sequel login, and vendor H R's.

109
00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:48.094
And look at payroll web's two strings:
the archive database already uses Active

110
00:06:48.094 --> 00:06:53.690
Directory Managed Identity, and the payroll
database still uses integrated security.

111
00:06:57.490 --> 00:07:01.440
Step three parses a string that
uses the deprecated password mode.

112
00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:04.352
It parses as entra, because
it is an Entra mode.

113
00:07:04.352 --> 00:07:06.478
But the mode is the deprecated one,

114
00:07:06.478 --> 00:07:09.665
so the scan reports it as a
finding, and never as done.

115
00:07:09.665 --> 00:07:11.971
A careless scan would count it as done,

116
00:07:11.971 --> 00:07:15.840
and a deprecated flow that can't
do M F A would stay in production.

117
00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:20.603
The kit's driver configuration lists the
deprecated modes, and the finding names it.

118
00:07:20.603 --> 00:07:24.740
And notice the password: even
here, it comes back masked.

119
00:07:28.540 --> 00:07:32.865
Step four reads the driver table the scan
uses: the first release of each mode,

120
00:07:32.865 --> 00:07:37.190
as the data platform book's chapter on
connection string migration gives it.

121
00:07:37.190 --> 00:07:41.390
Microsoft Data SQL Client has
managed identity from two point one.

122
00:07:41.390 --> 00:07:45.636
That's why payroll web, on five
point two point two, is column A.

123
00:07:45.636 --> 00:07:51.356
O D B C has managed identity from seventeen
point three point one point one, so the

124
00:07:51.356 --> 00:07:56.902
reports application's O D B C thirteen
driver has no Entra path, and it's column B.

125
00:07:56.902 --> 00:07:59.727
The last column is the
credential chain mode.

126
00:07:59.727 --> 00:08:03.510
Microsoft lists Default from three
point oh in the sequel client,

127
00:08:03.510 --> 00:08:05.510
and the kit never suggests it.

128
00:08:09.310 --> 00:08:15.466
Step five opens the manifest: the step,
its mode, read-only, its controls,

129
00:08:15.466 --> 00:08:21.298
I A five and C M six, its two K S Is,
and the S H A two fifty-six of each file.

130
00:08:21.298 --> 00:08:24.010
The kit wrote its own
output and rewrote nothing.

131
00:08:24.010 --> 00:08:28.185
Step six runs this chapter's
tests: seven pass, none fail.

132
00:08:28.185 --> 00:08:30.060
And step seven lists your runs.

133
00:08:30.060 --> 00:08:33.469
There's nothing to clean up: the
kit's folder is as you unzipped it,

134
00:08:33.469 --> 00:08:36.270
and each run's results
stay in their own folder.

135
00:08:40.070 --> 00:08:44.158
Lab 2 changes one string in one
test application, never production.

136
00:08:44.158 --> 00:08:46.058
It was not run by the authors.

137
00:08:46.058 --> 00:08:50.453
It must be a column-A case: an I I S
application on an Arc-enabled Windows host,

138
00:08:50.453 --> 00:08:55.496
using Microsoft Data SQL Client two point
one or later with integrated security,

139
00:08:55.496 --> 00:08:58.378
against a test instance
on sequel server twenty

140
00:08:58.378 --> 00:09:01.908
twenty-two or later with Entra
authentication configured.

141
00:09:01.908 --> 00:09:03.795
Its owner writes the app file.

142
00:09:03.795 --> 00:09:06.618
You copy the configuration
and scan the copy,

143
00:09:06.618 --> 00:09:10.433
then check gates one and two: the
login exists and holds a role.

144
00:09:10.433 --> 00:09:13.118
You let the application
pool ask for the token,

145
00:09:13.118 --> 00:09:16.794
keep the original file as the way
back, and change the one string.

146
00:09:16.794 --> 00:09:20.420
Gate three is seeing the application
arrive on its Entra login.

147
00:09:20.420 --> 00:09:23.732
Then a second scan should say
Entra already, column done.

148
00:09:23.732 --> 00:09:27.281
Step nine backs it all out: it
restores the original file,

149
00:09:27.281 --> 00:09:31.750
restarts the pool, removes it from
the group and signs out of Azure.

150
00:09:35.550 --> 00:09:37.150
The federal requirement.

151
00:09:37.150 --> 00:09:41.761
Chapter 07 of the workbook maps both
the scan and the change to I A five,

152
00:09:41.761 --> 00:09:49.775
Authenticator Management, and C M six,
Configuration Settings, in NIST SP 800-53.

153
00:09:49.775 --> 00:09:54.437
The scan supports I A five: a stored
password found, then removed.

154
00:09:54.437 --> 00:09:56.687
And it provides evidence for C M six:

155
00:09:56.687 --> 00:09:59.788
each string's authentication
setting, recorded.

156
00:09:59.788 --> 00:10:02.968
For the scan, the I S S O
keeps the connections file with

157
00:10:02.968 --> 00:10:06.800
masked strings and the manifest,
and each application's app file.

158
00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:09.139
For a change, the gate queries' output,

159
00:10:09.139 --> 00:10:13.350
the before and after scans, the
session read, and the change record.

160
00:10:13.350 --> 00:10:17.087
Runbook R oh eight lists the evidence
for each state of the cutover.

161
00:10:17.087 --> 00:10:21.316
K S Is are FedRAMP twenty X indicators
for cloud service providers;

162
00:10:21.316 --> 00:10:24.776
your providers report against
them, the agency doesn't.

163
00:10:24.776 --> 00:10:27.204
The scan's records support these controls;

164
00:10:27.204 --> 00:10:30.350
the assessor decides whether
each one is in place.

165
00:10:34.150 --> 00:10:36.200
Check yourself with two questions.

166
00:10:36.200 --> 00:10:40.475
First: payroll web's web dot
config uses integrated security.

167
00:10:40.475 --> 00:10:42.562
Why does the scan put it in column A?

168
00:10:42.562 --> 00:10:47.850
The answer is b: it runs on an Arc host
with Microsoft Data SQL Client five point

169
00:10:47.850 --> 00:10:51.402
two point two, at or above
the managed identity release,

170
00:10:51.402 --> 00:10:53.849
so the move is a configuration change.

171
00:10:53.849 --> 00:10:58.325
Second: which three conditions must hold
before a connection string changes?

172
00:10:58.325 --> 00:11:02.238
The answer is a: the Entra login
exists, it holds the database role,

173
00:11:02.238 --> 00:11:06.225
and the change is tested in a
production-equivalent environment.

174
00:11:06.225 --> 00:11:10.880
The module page has two more questions,
each linked to the section that answers it.

175
00:11:14.680 --> 00:11:15.705
To sum up.

176
00:11:15.705 --> 00:11:18.485
You scanned four fictional applications,

177
00:11:18.485 --> 00:11:22.447
found two stored passwords
without writing either one out,

178
00:11:22.447 --> 00:11:27.380
and placed each string in column A, B, C
or done, from its driver, host and owner.

179
00:11:27.380 --> 00:11:31.205
The scan advises; no string
changes until the three gates hold.

180
00:11:31.205 --> 00:11:35.837
The I S S O keeps the connections files
with masked strings and the manifest,

181
00:11:35.837 --> 00:11:40.021
each application's app file, and for
a change, the gate queries' output,

182
00:11:40.021 --> 00:11:44.055
the before and after scans, the
session read and the change record.

183
00:11:44.055 --> 00:11:47.605
Before shipping, work through
the Chapter 07 checklist.

184
00:11:47.605 --> 00:11:51.130
Next is Module 8, A D C S dependencies.
